---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ContactCTA from '../components/ContactCTA.astro';
const steps=[['Understand the business','I start by listening: how the team works, what leaders need to see, and where the operational friction shows up. The goal is a shared understanding of what matters most.'],['Agree on priorities','Together, we identify the work that deserves attention and define what progress should look like. The scope and working rhythm should fit your business.'],['Work alongside the team','I participate in leadership conversations, help coordinate across functions, and take responsibility for moving agreed priorities forward.'],['Build a useful rhythm','We review progress, make decisions with better information, and adjust as the business changes. The focus stays on practical improvements the team can sustain.']];
---
<Layout title="How Fractional RevOps Leadership Works" description="What a fractional Revenue Operations leader does, who benefits, and how embedded, part-time RevOps leadership differs from project consulting."><section class="wrap page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h1>Inside the team.<br/>Close to the work.</h1><p class="text-xl">Experienced leadership, without another full-time executive role.</p></section><section class="section bg-[#eef1ee]"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">What fractional leadership means</p><h2 class="max-w-3xl">An experienced RevOps leader, part of your team.</h2><div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-9 mt-12"><article class="focus"><h3>What I help with</h3><p>Forecasting and pipeline discipline, sales processes, reporting and analytics, CRM and sales technology priorities, and coordination across sales, finance, and other teams. The exact focus depends on where the business needs leadership most.</p></article><article class="focus"><h3>Who it's for</h3><p>Growing B2B businesses with operational challenges that need sustained attention, but where a full-time Revenue Operations executive may not be the right next hire. I work best with teams that want a partner who contributes directly.</p></article><article class="focus"><h3>How it's different</h3><p>A traditional consulting project may end with findings and recommendations. An embedded fractional relationship goes further: I stay involved in prioritizing, making decisions, coordinating the work, and following through alongside the people responsible.</p></article></div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 md:gap-24"><div><h2>A working partnership.</h2><p>I work with one or two companies at a time as an embedded, part-time member of their leadership teams. This is an ongoing relationship, with room to understand the business and contribute consistently.</p><p>The right engagement depends on what you need. We agree on priorities, responsibilities, and a working rhythm together.</p><p class="quote mt-10">I'm there to help do the work, not just tell you what needs to be done.</p></div><div>{steps.map(([title,body],i)=><article class="border-t border-[#cbd4d8] py-7"><p class="eyebrow mb-3">0{i+1}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div></div></section><ContactCTA/></Layout>
